          <toggle-section open="false">
            <h3>Video transcript </h3>
            <p><strong>Chris:</strong> So, what exactly is Kanban and how does it work?</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Kanban first came about in Japan. The Japanese word ‘Kanban’ translates as ‘signboard’ or ‘visual card’. In the 1940s it was developed [with LEAN methodology] by an engineer for Toyota. It was predominately used in ‘just-in-time’ manufacturing. Kanban came to the attention of the software industry and is nowadays used in the Agile methodology. The objective of Kanban is to simplify planning, manage work and inventory, and increase productivity and efficiency.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Kanban is a visual system, and the flow of work is managed using a Kanban project board. Often these same types of project boards are used in Agile and Scrum methodology. In its simplest form there are three columns which are labelled: ‘To Do’, ‘In Progress’, and ‘Done’. These labels would be different depending on the type of project, so for example a Scrum Kanban board could have more columns and could include Stories, Current iteration, In Progress, Tested, and Delivered.</p>
<p>So how does this work in practice?</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> A task is put on a card and placed on the board. Each card has a priority and a team member.</p>
<p>As the task is assigned, worked on, and completed, it is moved from one column to the next.</p>
<p>As the cards move across the board, you can identify issues at each stage of the process.</p>
<p>When all task cards are in the ‘Done’ column, the iteration is marked as complete.</p>
<p>There are 4 principles of Kanban, so, let’s have a look at these.</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> Visualise workflow</p>
<p>The Kanban board is the project visual workflow as in there would be three columns e.g. To do, In Progress, and Done [as we have already mentioned], with coloured cards for each task. These would be assigned to team members and moved according to their current status.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> Limit work in progress [WIP]</p>
<p>This is where the individual tasks would be prioritised, assigned a certain amount of ‘points’ [time to take/complexity], and there would be a limit on how many tasks [workload] each team member would have.</p>
<p>Focus on Flow</p>
<p><strong>Chris:</strong> The workflow should run smoothly, but stoppages could be used as opportunities for improvement. Measurements may be used to track and analyse such as ‘Lead time’, ‘cycle time’ and ‘workload’.</p>
<p><strong>Leah:</strong> And finally Continuous improvement</p>
<p>The results and the Kanban system would be monitored to determine if it works, and if not something else should be tried.</p>

          </toggle-section>
        
      
          
